::scene TITLE

ROOM studio_stage

EVENT FADE_IN

TITLE halcyon_reyou_logo

♪ Three chrome tones, warm and unhurried, the kind of sound a waiting room plays at you until you agree. ♪

::scene COMMERCIAL

CAM 1 WIDE reyou_print_floor

NPC npc_narrator

SHOT
A white room with no corners. A row of tanks, softly lit, each one holding a person who is not currently needed.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
Everybody gets one life. That was the arrangement. Nobody was asked, and nobody has ever been happy with theirs.

NARRATOR:
So Halcyon changed the arrangement.

CAM 2 CLOSEUP tank_glass

SHOT
A hand presses to the glass from the inside. It is a very good hand. It has never held anything, yet.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
Halcyon Re-You™. We take your impression now, while you're standing there breathing and signing things.

NARRATOR:
And on the day it all goes wrong out there — and it does go wrong out there — you open your eyes on our floor, whole, warm, and owed a second go at it.

NARRATOR:
Not a copy. Not a keepsake. A second chance, in your own face, with your own bad ideas still intact.

::scene TESTIMONIALS

CAM 3 MEDIUM testimonial_couch

SHOT
A beige couch that has never been in anybody's home. A woman sits on it with the posture of someone who has been told to relax.
SHOT_END

NPC npc_reyou_dessa

DESSA:
I remember getting caught out in the rain without a hood, and I remember thinking, well, that was stupid. And then I remember the ceiling here.

DESSA:
Nothing in between. No tunnel, no light, no relatives. Just a bit of a gap, like nodding off on a tram.

DESSA:
Best thing about it? I got my whole twenties back and I already know how they end.

CAM 4 MEDIUM testimonial_couch

SHOT
A man in a work jacket, hands folded, both of them noticeably newer than his face.
SHOT_END

ORRIN:
The lads had a bet on how I'd go. Nobody had "vending machine." I came back four hours later and settled it myself, which they did not enjoy.

ORRIN:
Walking home with no boots and no coat, that's the humbling part. Halcyon gives you your life back. Your trousers are between you and the city.

ACTION npc_reyou_orrin shrugs with the calm of a man who has done this twice

CAM 5 CLOSEUP testimonial_couch

SHOT
A young woman, delighted, sitting far too close to the lens.
SHOT_END

BEX:
Everyone goes on about how you'd live differently if you got another go. I've had another go. I do exactly the same things, just faster.

BEX:
I'm braver now! Not smarter. Nobody claimed smarter. But you try a thing, and it goes badly, and then you're up and you're trying it again by lunch.

BEX:
Honestly the worst part is finding out who kept your stuff.

CAM 6 MEDIUM testimonial_couch

SHOT
A very large man on a very small cushion, still faintly annoyed about something.
SHOT_END

TUCK:
Freight train. Through the Yards. And before anybody says anything — in my defence, it was a very long way off when I set out.

TUCK:
I could see it the whole time. It could see me the whole time. We both had ample opportunity to reconsider and neither of us took it.

TUCK:
Halcyon had me up by teatime, and I'll be honest, the walk home was worse than the train. Train was quick. Walk was forty minutes with no trousers past everyone I know.

ACTION npc_reyou_tuck stares into the middle distance, reliving a specific injustice

CAM 7 WIDE testimonial_couch

SHOT
An older woman on the same couch, alone, very composed, hands flat on her knees.
SHOT_END

MARGUERITE:
It wasn't me, it was Wallace. Thirty-one years married. He went out for a fuse and came back on the Thursday, no coat, no boots, very cheerful.

MARGUERITE:
He's left-handed now. Thirty-one years right-handed, and he comes back through that door buttering toast the wrong way about like it's nothing.

MARGUERITE:
He doesn't like fish. He's never not liked fish. And he's polite to me, which he wasn't, and which I've decided I'll allow.

MARGUERITE:
Can't remember our wedding. Can remember every word of a song neither of us ever liked. I've stopped asking. It only upsets the one of us who was there.

ACTION npc_reyou_marguerite adjusts her cardigan with enormous dignity

MARGUERITE:
No, I shan't be putting in a complaint. I've thought about it. He does the washing up now.

::scene CLOSE

CAM 8 MEDIUM claims_desk

NPC npc_narrator

SHOT
A Halcyon clerk stamps a form without looking up. Behind her, the calm eye watches the skyline, and the skyline watches nothing at all.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
So take the shortcut through the Yards. Say the thing to the man with the bat. Swim out a bit further than you should.

NARRATOR:
Halcyon Re-You™. Coverage begins the moment you stop, and so, thankfully, do you.

OVERLAY
HALCYON RE-YOU™

A SECOND CHANCE AT LIFE

WHEN THE FIRST TIME WASN'T GOOD ENOUGH
OVERLAY_END

TICKER
Halcyon Re-You™ activates on confirmed cessation only; deliberately triggering coverage is fraud, and is also, statistically, extremely popular · You return with your body, your memories and your debts, in that order of reliability · Impressions older than six months may omit recent relationships, grudges, PIN numbers and children · Halcyon returns the person, never the possessions; the city keeps whatever you were carrying and the city has always kept it · Personality drift of up to 11% falls within Halcyon's published tolerance and is not grounds for refund · In the unlikely event the original is recovered alive, Halcyon will contact both parties and requests that neither of you does anything hasty before we arrive · Halcyon has been watching you sleep for eleven years and has notes
TICKER_END

NARRATOR:
Halcyon Re-You™. When the first time wasn't good enough.

SHOT
The seal closes over the skyline like an eyelid, and holds, and does not blink.
SHOT_END

EVENT FADE_OUT

END
